Notes: 
The code snippet does a linear scan from end to the start so there are two assertions which could be problematic because the order of addition and removal are not deterministic. 

The third assert contains _items[OLD(index)] which could be incorrect when multiple remove calls are processed in a loop after a AddRange call because those modifications are not being included in the snapshot. 

For the array implementation of List, it maintains its own count that is constantly incremented every time adding an element, but we cannot use OLD to correctly predict the count of the next element, because in this codebase, the _size variable has been already modified by the RemoveAt call. We have no way to use OLD as the actual snapshot of _size is not available for sure in RemoveAt function, the version number is also not helpful, as it is incremented in any modification to the list. 

Without an assumption, it's difficult to provide a robust solution. If the list is not modified after the `AddRange` call, then the previous assertions are applicable.--------------------
public void RemoveAt(int index)
{
    Debug.Assert((index >= 0 && index < _size), "SR.ArgumentOutOfRange_Index");
    Array.Copy(_items, index+1, _items, index, _size - index - 1);
    _items[--_size] = null;
    _version++;
}
